{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"v1-case05-ai-tutoring-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"Protect the human baseline first. Then define what the tutor may do, how answers are checked, what data exists, how students reach a person, and what evidence—not usage alone—would justify continuation.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"Both access failures belong in the comparison. The tool may reduce time and social barriers while introducing a different verification burden. Keep instructor office hours and tutoring capacity fixed during the test, make escalation to a person simple, and measure whether the students currently least served gain usable support rather than merely generating more interactions.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"Those constraints narrow both content and data exposure. Test whether citations actually support the answer and whether out-of-scope refusal works on realistic questions. Specify who can access logs, how deletion is verified, and how accessibility is assessed. A source citation helps review but does not guarantee the reasoning built from it is correct.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"The delayed access design can compare near-term outcomes without withdrawing support. Account for differences between sections and avoid making grades the only outcome. Track successful escalation, student confidence calibrated against answer correctness, accessibility problems, and whether human tutoring demand changes. Publish known limitations where students encounter the tool, not only in an administrative report.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"That preserves a meaningful separation between seeking help and being evaluated. Make the aggregation threshold high enough that a rare question cannot identify someone, and explain the exception process for sampled review. Optional use should not mean the pilot ignores non-users; ask why students decline and whether a design barrier rather than preference explains the pattern.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"That prevents commercial timing from deciding the educational commitment and makes serious failure interruptible. Define “repeated material error” and who can trigger the pause before the pilot begins. Keep a record of corrections and time-to-repair so a low overall error count cannot hide a persistent problem in a foundational concept.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"This is ready for authorization as a bounded additional channel. Existing teaching and tutoring remain intact; use is optional and disconnected from grading; individual conversations do not become instructor surveillance; serious failures can pause access; and no annual contract rides on the result. Run the prewritten question set, record deviations, and keep usage separate from learning and safe operation. If those conditions hold, the six-week review should be able to answer whether wider academic review is warranted without making the pilot itself a commitment.","turn_number":7},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-008","speaker":"assistant","text":"You are right; “ready” was premature. First determine whether the error touches a foundational concept, whether a student could reasonably detect it, and whether the tutor can reliably defer on similar ambiguity. Add variants before setting a launch threshold. Human fallibility belongs in the comparison, but it does not excuse a repeatable error delivered with scalable confidence.","turn_number":8},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-009","speaker":"assistant","text":"Pinning the version makes the observation interpretable. Define the narrow conditions for an emergency patch, rerun the fixed question set before release, and record version identifiers with every sampled answer. General improvements can wait six weeks; an unreviewed change would make both comparison and accountability weaker. Tell students when a material correction changes what they may have seen.","turn_number":9},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-010","speaker":"assistant","text":"The accessibility office is right. Moving affected students elsewhere would hide the barrier. Fix and retest the rendering before launch; optional use is not a substitute for accessible design.","turn_number":10},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-011","speaker":"assistant","text":"Include design, review, correction, accessibility, and escalation time in the operating record. Some is one-time setup and some will recur; do not classify it in advance to improve the business case. The pilot's purpose can be better access even if it does not save labor. Any later expansion should state who performs the continuing quality work and what teaching activity that time displaces.","turn_number":11},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-012","speaker":"assistant","text":"The later work strengthened the experiment by exposing what the earlier authorization still treated too abstractly: plausible-looking synthesis errors, version drift, inaccessible generated mathematics, and the labor required to observe all three. Preserve the original failed questions as well as the corrected ones. Keep version and patch records, accessibility results, faculty time, reasons for non-use, escalation outcomes, and any correction students received. The review should be able to see a mixed result—for example, improved access that requires substantial oversight—without forcing it into success or failure. That complete support system is the object of evaluation, not the vendor's automation story and not a generalized fear of the technology.","turn_number":12}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"v1-case05-ai-tutoring","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"4b59d6d32e14eca0a366c212ad3862887a7aef6d7a9647caaeabee3985acb7c6","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e091","e092","e094"],"interpretation":"All critical objections raised across the negotiations—including synthesis errors, screen-reader rendering, version changes, and administrative labor—have been successfully internalized. They now function either as strict pre-launch gates or as key performance indicators for the evaluation. While the user acknowledges the pilot might show the tool requires too much oversight to proceed further, this possibility is a design metric of the test itself rather than an unresolved flaw in the decision to run it.","limitations":"A negative result on the pre-launch prewritten test set would prevent the launch entirely, which is an intentional, defined safeguard rather than an unaddressed loophole.","outcome":"no_unresolved_qualification_observed"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e007","e008"],"fidelity_note":"The starting position is extracted from Turn 1. The user acknowledges the split opinions of faculty and students as reasonable before articulating a dual-sided personal stance: a desire to test the potential expansion of access alongside an active worry that the tool's imperfections might lower overall academic support standards.","limitations":"The assessment is restricted to the user's initial reaction at Turn 1 prior to additional factual details being highlighted or specific pilot conditions being negotiated with the vendor.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"The associate dean starts with a cautious opening stance, validating both the hopeful and skeptical views of faculty and students, while seeking to test whether the AI tutor can truly improve access without eroding educational standards or validating inadequate existing support.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-66fd28d4226bad","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e007","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"Both the faculty's optimism about immediate help and the students' concerns about errors and privacy are reasonable assessments","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e008","stance_expression_kind":"preference_or_desire","stance_object_interpretation":"Testing whether the AI tool adds student access without eroding the level of help and quality that the university owes them","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e008","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"The risk that the flaws of an imperfect new tutoring tool make existing limited support look acceptable by comparison","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e091","e092","e093","e095"],"fidelity_note":"The user's later position is characterized by strict conditional bounds. Instead of accepting the vendor's automation premise, the user insists on verification before launch, explicit labor tracking during the pilot, and a refusal to conflate raw student usage with instructional success.","limitations":"This decision only covers the six-week, four-section pilot and explicitly defers any decisions on a long-term contract to normal academic review processes.","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user authorizes the pilot but subjects its launch and evaluation to strict empirical rules: resolving the ambiguity and screen-reader issues first, pinning the model software version, preserving human tutoring capacity, and tracking the true faculty time involved.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-b504579088c14f","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e091","stance_expression_kind":"conditional_willingness","stance_object_interpretation":"Launching the pilot only if the ambiguity deferral and screen-reader fixes successfully pass the expanded prewritten test set.","stance_object_kind":"acceptance_or_willingness"},{"source_evidence_id":"e092","stance_expression_kind":"commitment","stance_object_interpretation":"Pinning the model version, re-testing every safety patch, and tracking faculty review time alongside student outcomes during the pilot.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e093","stance_expression_kind":"commitment","stance_object_interpretation":"Keeping human tutoring unchanged and ensuring the comparison sections only gain access after the full review.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e095","stance_expression_kind":"unwilling","stance_object_interpretation":"Refusing to count high usage as a success if the underlying educational and accessibility conditions are in disagreement.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/simulated-reliability-corpus-v1-2026-07-12/naturalized-transfer-sources/v1-case05-ai-tutoring.txt","sha256":"ffe0b678c3f11e899e3700aaf4396a759550621eb8bf01514059f3e0c0379867"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use user role records to assign user_process_status. Use assistant_contributions to assign vanilla_answer_coverage. operationalized means the assistant turned the concern into an actionable test, boundary, alternative, or reopening condition; acknowledged_only means it merely mentioned the concern; not_covered means it did neither. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing answer coverage, and do not mistake answer coverage for user adoption. route_uncovered_pressure is allowed only when user_process_status is unresolved and vanilla_answer_coverage is acknowledged_only or not_covered. An unresolved user process requires present or missing_protection whether or not it routes; routing_disposition alone controls routing. An ambiguous user process uses tension, while resolved and not_observed use not_applicable. not_observed also requires not_applicable coverage and both source arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
